put(39, 'CyberForge is a web-threat analysis and user-protection research system that combines specialised analysers, contextual evidence and human-readable explanations. The original prototype coordinated eight specialist agents through a three-stage directed acyclic graph, followed by a Reporter. Its reported evaluation on 300 URLs (150 benign and 150 harmful) yielded 94.7% accuracy, 97.9% precision, 91.3% recall and a 2.0% false-positive rate, with mean end-to-end latency of 4.05 seconds. These are retained as historical prototype results, not measurements of the revised implementation. A source review dated 18 October 2026 identifies the current implementation as a Tauri 2/Rust desktop, a Node.js 24/Express backend, a FastAPI Hugging Face service, a shared Rust core, a scoped CLI and a grant-controlled stdio MCP adapter. The ML service combines supervised gradient-boosted URL classifiers, unsupervised IsolationForest scoring, externally pretrained BERT and Qwen inference, and explicit heuristic fallbacks. Current controls include consent-based collection, authenticated workspace access, device enrollment and signed commands, evidence records, and capability-aware status reporting. Memory supplies retrieval context; it does not update model weights. The current implementation requires fresh held-out evaluation and packaged/live-service acceptance before historical performance can be attributed to it.')
put(43, 'CyberForge est un système de recherche consacré à l’analyse des menaces web et à la protection des utilisateurs. Le prototype initial coordonnait huit agents spécialisés dans un graphe orienté acyclique en trois étapes, suivi d’un agent rapporteur. L’évaluation historique de 300 URL (150 légitimes et 150 malveillantes) rapporte une exactitude de 94,7 %, une précision de 97,9 %, un rappel de 91,3 %, un taux de faux positifs de 2,0 % et une latence moyenne de 4,05 secondes. Ces résultats ne constituent pas une nouvelle évaluation du système révisé. La revue du code du 18 octobre 2026 identifie un client Tauri 2/Rust, un backend Node.js 24/Express, un service FastAPI sur Hugging Face, un noyau Rust partagé, une interface en ligne de commande et un adaptateur MCP stdio soumis à autorisation. Le service ML associe classification supervisée, détection non supervisée d’anomalies, inférence avec BERT et Qwen préentraînés et heuristiques explicitement identifiées. La collecte dépend du consentement; les actions et les données sont soumises à des contrôles d’accès. La mémoire conserve du contexte sans réentraîner les modèles. Une nouvelle évaluation indépendante et des essais de la version installée restent nécessaires avant de généraliser les résultats historiques à cette version.')
put(159, 'Historical observations are retained as contextual evidence for later analysis. This memory and retrieval mechanism is distinct from learning model parameters. Current collection and cloud processing are independently consent-gated. The original URL experiment is reported in Chapter 4; the October 2026 source revision is documented in Chapter 3 and Appendix C and has not been assigned new detection-performance figures.')
put(165, 'CyberForge addresses these problems through specialist analysis, inspectable evidence and controlled sharing of results. The original eight-agent analysis workflow remains distinguishable from the newer durable agent-run framework: the latter currently exposes URL Analyst and Device Health templates with bounded tools, while broader autonomous planning remains future work.')
put(184, 'Academically, the contribution is the integration and orchestration of complementary evidence sources rather than a claim that every component is a trained AI model. The current system includes supervised URL classifiers, an unsupervised anomaly detector, pretrained language/URL models and deterministic extraction rules. Their outputs, failure states and provenance must be distinguished. Reproducibility depends on recording source revisions, dataset splits, feature definitions and model metadata, not merely publishing application code.')
put(185, 'The historical development study identified and removed an additive +0.5 risk-score offset. Its small calibration set is distinct from the 300-URL evaluation. Neither a model confidence value nor successful calibration of a few selected URLs establishes calibrated probability or out-of-sample performance. The revised implementation explicitly labels unavailable engines and heuristic responses.')
put(186, 'The system uses modular client, backend, inference and persistence services. The backend configuration supports a configured deployment origin and the ML_SERVICE_URL inference destination; the Hugging Face deployment source is ml-services/hf_space_deploy. Heroku and Appwrite remain present in project configuration, alongside MongoDB control-plane persistence. This source review does not certify current provider availability, hosting capacity or production deployment parity.')
put(189, 'The scope is URL and web-context security: analysing submitted or consent-collected addresses, examining available page signals, retrieving intelligence and presenting evidence-based warnings. Supported domain-blocking actions are mediated by native permissions or approved signed device commands. A request being queued or delivered is not proof that blocking was applied.')
put(191, 'The analysis workspace presents available URL, page and threat-intelligence evidence. It is not a virtual-machine malware detonation environment and does not establish arbitrary binary analysis or comprehensive endpoint protection. Observation coverage depends on supported sensors, permissions, consent and service availability.')
